1097
00:42:23,440 --> 00:42:27,599
By applying gradients step by step, we

1098
00:42:25,599 --> 00:42:29,680
gradually eliminate one dimension at a

1099
00:42:27,599 --> 00:42:31,839
time. Slice selection removes the Z

1100
00:42:29,680 --> 00:42:33,839
dimension, phase encoding handles the Y

1101
00:42:31,839 --> 00:42:36,160
dimension, and frequency encoding takes

1102
00:42:33,839 --> 00:42:38,240
care of the X dimension. The end result

1103
00:42:36,160 --> 00:42:40,720
is three-dimensional point specific

1104
00:42:38,240 --> 00:42:43,760
localization of the MR signal. This is

1105
00:42:40,720 --> 00:42:45,760
the foundation of tomographic imaging.

1106
00:42:43,760 --> 00:42:48,400
Mathematically, we can describe this

1107
00:42:45,760 --> 00:42:50,400
process using the equations shown here.

1108
00:42:48,400 --> 00:42:52,319
If we only apply frequency encoding

1109
00:42:50,400 --> 00:42:54,480
without phase encoding, the acquired

1110
00:42:52,319 --> 00:42:56,720
signal is expressed as an integral over

1111
00:42:54,480 --> 00:43:01,280
the slice that includes a factor of e to

1112
00:42:56,720 --> 00:43:02,800
the minus j gamma gx * x * t.

1113
00:43:01,280 --> 00:43:05,119
This captures spatial frequency

1114
00:43:02,800 --> 00:43:07,520
information along the x direction. But

1115
00:43:05,119 --> 00:43:09,760
when we combine both phase encoding and

1116
00:43:07,520 --> 00:43:11,920
frequency encoding, the signal equation

1117
00:43:09,760 --> 00:43:14,800
becomes the one you see at the bottom of

1118
00:43:11,920 --> 00:43:18,319
the slide. Here you have an additional

1119
00:43:14,800 --> 00:43:20,880
factor of e to the minus j gamma g y * y

1120
00:43:18,319 --> 00:43:22,960
* p. This accounts for the phase

1121
00:43:20,880 --> 00:43:26,560
encoding gradient applied in the y

1122
00:43:22,960 --> 00:43:28,480
direction for a certain time to p.

1123
00:43:26,560 --> 00:43:30,319
Now looking at this combined equation

1124
00:43:28,480 --> 00:43:32,400
you can see it resembles a forier

1125
00:43:30,319 --> 00:43:34,640
transformation. You have terms in both

1126
00:43:32,400 --> 00:43:37,680
the x and y directions frequency terms

1127
00:43:34,640 --> 00:43:39,520
in x and phase terms in y.

1128
00:43:37,680 --> 00:43:41,200
Together they allow us to map spatial

1129
00:43:39,520 --> 00:43:43,520
coordinates into measurable signal

1130
00:43:41,200 --> 00:43:46,400
space. This is the mathematical backbone

1131
00:43:43,520 --> 00:43:48,720
of MRI imaging. We will go deeper into

1132
00:43:46,400 --> 00:43:50,400
this forier interpretation later in the

1133
00:43:48,720 --> 00:43:52,079
lecture.

1134
00:43:50,400 --> 00:43:53,920
But for now, recognize that the

1135
00:43:52,079 --> 00:43:56,000
combination of slice selection, phase

1136
00:43:53,920 --> 00:43:58,079
encoding, and frequency encoding

1137
00:43:56,000 --> 00:44:00,400
provides us with true point-wise

1138
00:43:58,079 --> 00:44:02,319
information. Graphically, as shown here,

1139
00:44:00,400 --> 00:44:05,319
this is exactly how the imaging sequence

1140
00:44:02,319 --> 00:44:05,319
works.

1141
00:44:06,560 --> 00:44:10,319
So here we come to the full sequence

1142
00:44:08,400 --> 00:44:12,800
that combines slice selection, phase

1143
00:44:10,319 --> 00:44:16,240
encoding and frequency encoding. The

1144
00:44:12,800 --> 00:44:18,240
process begins with a 90° pulse.

1145
00:44:16,240 --> 00:44:20,400
This radio frequency pulse flips the

1146
00:44:18,240 --> 00:44:23,359
magnetization vector, the big M vector

1147
00:44:20,400 --> 00:44:25,359
into the XY plane. Once it is in the XY

1148
00:44:23,359 --> 00:44:27,040
plane, the transverse components begin

1149
00:44:25,359 --> 00:44:29,200
to process and these processing

1150
00:44:27,040 --> 00:44:31,680
components induce an alternating

1151
00:44:29,200 --> 00:44:34,000
magnetic field around the patient or the

1152
00:44:31,680 --> 00:44:36,640
sample. That alternating field is what

1153
00:44:34,000 --> 00:44:38,319
we measure as the MR signal.

1154
00:44:36,640 --> 00:44:40,720
Now you want to make sure that you are

1155
00:44:38,319 --> 00:44:42,400
not flipping every spin in the body. You

1156
00:44:40,720 --> 00:44:44,560
only want to flip the spins within a

1157
00:44:42,400 --> 00:44:47,040
single slice. That is where slice

1158
00:44:44,560 --> 00:44:48,800
selection comes in. During the 90°

1159
00:44:47,040 --> 00:44:50,319
pulse, you apply a slice selection

1160
00:44:48,800 --> 00:44:52,319
gradient.

1161
00:44:50,319 --> 00:44:53,920
This gradient ensures that only the

1162
00:44:52,319 --> 00:44:55,920
spins that are resonant within that

1163
00:44:53,920 --> 00:44:58,480
slice frequency range are flipped into

1164
00:44:55,920 --> 00:45:00,160
the XY plane. Spins outside the slice

1165
00:44:58,480 --> 00:45:02,400
are not flipped and so they do not

1166
00:45:00,160 --> 00:45:04,319
contribute to the signal. This is the

1167
00:45:02,400 --> 00:45:06,319
first major step. Slice selection

1168
00:45:04,319 --> 00:45:09,040
removes one dimension narrowing the

1169
00:45:06,319 --> 00:45:11,520
signal to a single slice.

1170
00:45:09,040 --> 00:45:13,680
Next, you apply phase encoding. On the

1171
00:45:11,520 --> 00:45:16,319
slide, you see these multiple horizontal

1172
00:45:13,680 --> 00:45:18,480
lines under the label G-phase. This is

1173
00:45:16,319 --> 00:45:20,640
because phase encoding must be repeated

1174
00:45:18,480 --> 00:45:22,800
multiple times with different gradient

1175
00:45:20,640 --> 00:45:24,800
strengths. You can start from a minimum

1176
00:45:22,800 --> 00:45:26,640
value, then gradually increase step by

1177
00:45:24,800 --> 00:45:29,119
step, covering a full range of gradient

1178
00:45:26,640 --> 00:45:31,200
amplitudes. Each phase encoding step

1179
00:45:29,119 --> 00:45:33,520
imprints a different spatially dependent

1180
00:45:31,200 --> 00:45:38,000
phase shift onto the spins along the y

1181
00:45:33,520 --> 00:45:40,400
direction. Why do we need so many lines?

1182
00:45:38,000 --> 00:45:42,560
The reason is the forier transformation.

1183
00:45:40,400 --> 00:45:44,240
To fully reconstruct an image, we need

1184
00:45:42,560 --> 00:45:46,400
information along two degrees of

1185
00:45:44,240 --> 00:45:48,560
freedom. One for the phase dimension and

1186
00:45:46,400 --> 00:45:50,400
one for the frequency dimension. Each

1187
00:45:48,560 --> 00:45:52,960
phase encoding line is like collecting

1188
00:45:50,400 --> 00:45:55,040
one row of data in forier space which we

1189
00:45:52,960 --> 00:45:57,200
call k space.

1190
00:45:55,040 --> 00:45:59,200
After that we apply frequency encoding

1191
00:45:57,200 --> 00:46:01,359
shown here as G frack. Frequency

1192
00:45:59,200 --> 00:46:03,599
encoding happens during the readout.

1193
00:46:01,359 --> 00:46:05,119
With this gradient on different spatial

1194
00:46:03,599 --> 00:46:06,640
locations along the X direction

1195
00:46:05,119 --> 00:46:08,480
correspond to different precession

1196
00:46:06,640 --> 00:46:10,560
frequencies.

1197
00:46:08,480 --> 00:46:12,720
When you record the signal you collect a

1198
00:46:10,560 --> 00:46:15,440
series of oscillations that map into the

1199
00:46:12,720 --> 00:46:17,280
X-axis of K space. Each readout provides

1200
00:46:15,440 --> 00:46:19,440
you with n data points along this

1201
00:46:17,280 --> 00:46:21,599
horizontal axis.

1202
00:46:19,440 --> 00:46:23,680
So putting everything together for each

1203
00:46:21,599 --> 00:46:26,000
phase encoding step you collect one

1204
00:46:23,680 --> 00:46:27,920
frequency encoded readout with n data

1205
00:46:26,000 --> 00:46:29,839
points. After repeating this process

1206
00:46:27,920 --> 00:46:32,079
with many different phase encoding steps

1207
00:46:29,839 --> 00:46:34,160
you end up with a full grid of data n

1208
00:46:32,079 --> 00:46:36,319
byn points.

1209
00:46:34,160 --> 00:46:38,400
Each row comes from one phase encoding

1210
00:46:36,319 --> 00:46:41,200
and each column comes from the frequency

1211
00:46:38,400 --> 00:46:43,040
encoding readout. Altogether this maps

1212
00:46:41,200 --> 00:46:45,280
the entire cross-section of the slice

1213
00:46:43,040 --> 00:46:47,520
into kspace. Then by applying a

1214
00:46:45,280 --> 00:46:50,160
two-dimensional forier transform, you

1215
00:46:47,520 --> 00:46:52,319
reconstruct the final image.

1216
00:46:50,160 --> 00:46:54,319
This is the essence of forier imaging or

1217
00:46:52,319 --> 00:46:56,000
what we call casebased formulation in

1218
00:46:54,319 --> 00:46:58,240
MRI.

1219
00:46:56,000 --> 00:47:00,400
Slice selection removes one dimension,

1220
00:46:58,240 --> 00:47:02,480
phase encoding handles the Y dimension

1221
00:47:00,400 --> 00:47:04,640
and frequency encoding handles the X

1222
00:47:02,480 --> 00:47:06,560
dimension. The result is a complete

1223
00:47:04,640 --> 00:47:10,839
two-dimensional image of the selected

1224
00:47:06,560 --> 00:47:10,839
slice pixel by pixel.

1225
00:47:12,160 --> 00:47:18,400
So if we call the whole term for

1226
00:47:14,000 --> 00:47:21,200
frequency encoding gx * t simply kx and

1227
00:47:18,400 --> 00:47:23,280
we call the phase encoding term ky then

1228
00:47:21,200 --> 00:47:26,400
the previous formula we derived can be

1229
00:47:23,280 --> 00:47:27,920
rewritten in this kspace format

1230
00:47:26,400 --> 00:47:31,040
the signal is proportional to the

1231
00:47:27,920 --> 00:47:32,560
factors kx * x and ky * y and this

1232
00:47:31,040 --> 00:47:34,400
immediately shows that the signal we

1233
00:47:32,560 --> 00:47:36,319
measure is essentially a forier

1234
00:47:34,400 --> 00:47:38,640
transformation

1235
00:47:36,319 --> 00:47:40,319
for any fixed time point during readout

1236
00:47:38,640 --> 00:47:42,960
the time t sets a specific specific

1237
00:47:40,319 --> 00:47:45,040
value of kx while ky is determined by

1238
00:47:42,960 --> 00:47:47,359
the strength of the gradient gy and the

1239
00:47:45,040 --> 00:47:49,359
duration of the phase encoding step to

1240
00:47:47,359 --> 00:47:51,839
p. In other words, the frequency

1241
00:47:49,359 --> 00:47:53,920
encoding factor is traced out by letting

1242
00:47:51,839 --> 00:47:56,319
time run during readout which sweeps

1243
00:47:53,920 --> 00:47:58,640
continuously along one line in k space

1244
00:47:56,319 --> 00:48:01,200
while the phase encoding only gives one

1245
00:47:58,640 --> 00:48:04,400
value of ky per line. And this is why we

1246
00:48:01,200 --> 00:48:06,160
need multiple phase encoding steps.

1247
00:48:04,400 --> 00:48:08,240
By repeating the experiment with many

1248
00:48:06,160 --> 00:48:10,560
different gradient strengths for gy, we

1249
00:48:08,240 --> 00:48:12,800
can step through multiple values of KY

1250
00:48:10,560 --> 00:48:15,040
gradually filling the two-dimensional k

1251
00:48:12,800 --> 00:48:16,640
space. This is the basis of forier

1252
00:48:15,040 --> 00:48:18,560
imaging.

1253
00:48:16,640 --> 00:48:21,839
On the slide, you can see the formal

1254
00:48:18,560 --> 00:48:26,079
definitions written explicitly kx= gamma

1255
00:48:21,839 --> 00:48:29,760
/ 2<unk>i * gx * t and ky = gamma over

1256
00:48:26,079 --> 00:48:31,599
2<unk>i * gy * p. Substituting these

1257
00:48:29,760 --> 00:48:34,079
into the signal equation, we get that s

1258
00:48:31,599 --> 00:48:37,280
of kx ky is proportional to the double

1259
00:48:34,079 --> 00:48:41,680
integral over the slice of rho of x y *

1260
00:48:37,280 --> 00:48:45,280
eus j 2<unk>i * open parenthesis kxx + k

1261
00:48:41,680 --> 00:48:48,000
y y close parenthesis dx dy. This is

1262
00:48:45,280 --> 00:48:50,079
shown as equation 4.49 and it is the

1263
00:48:48,000 --> 00:48:51,680
central mathematical statement of forier

1264
00:48:50,079 --> 00:48:53,839
MRI.

1265
00:48:51,680 --> 00:48:56,000
Notice that in your textbook the 2PI

1266
00:48:53,839 --> 00:48:57,839
factor is missing inside the exponential

1267
00:48:56,000 --> 00:49:00,079
and this is actually a common oversight

1268
00:48:57,839 --> 00:49:02,960
in many texts including mine and in my

1269
00:49:00,079 --> 00:49:04,720
lectures. In reality the 2PI must be

1270
00:49:02,960 --> 00:49:07,200
included to match the conventional

1271
00:49:04,720 --> 00:49:09,119
definition of the forier transform. So

1272
00:49:07,200 --> 00:49:11,040
keep that in mind as a small but

1273
00:49:09,119 --> 00:49:12,880
important detail.

1274
00:49:11,040 --> 00:49:15,040
The essential point here is that through

1275
00:49:12,880 --> 00:49:17,520
phase encoding and frequency encoding

1276
00:49:15,040 --> 00:49:20,000
the data we measure in MRI are already

1277
00:49:17,520 --> 00:49:22,079
furer components. This is very different

1278
00:49:20,000 --> 00:49:24,640
from X-ray imaging where the Furier

1279
00:49:22,079 --> 00:49:26,319
slice theorem has to be used. There each

1280
00:49:24,640 --> 00:49:28,480
projection profile must be

1281
00:49:26,319 --> 00:49:31,040
one-dimensionally transformed to draw a

1282
00:49:28,480 --> 00:49:34,480
radial line in Furier space and extra

1283
00:49:31,040 --> 00:49:36,800
processing is needed. In MRI the signals

1284
00:49:34,480 --> 00:49:38,800
themselves directly populate kspace

1285
00:49:36,800 --> 00:49:40,559
which means the forier information is

1286
00:49:38,800 --> 00:49:42,079
collected naturally as part of the

1287
00:49:40,559 --> 00:49:44,160
measurement.

1288
00:49:42,079 --> 00:49:46,400
Each phase encoding step contributes to

1289
00:49:44,160 --> 00:49:48,240
the vertical direction in kspace and the

1290
00:49:46,400 --> 00:49:50,240
continuous readout under the frequency

1291
00:49:48,240 --> 00:49:52,880
encoding gradient sweeps across the

1292
00:49:50,240 --> 00:49:54,960
horizontal direction. By combining both

1293
00:49:52,880 --> 00:49:57,440
we directly fill kspace and after

1294
00:49:54,960 --> 00:50:01,559
applying the inverse furier transform we

1295
00:49:57,440 --> 00:50:01,559
reconstruct the final image.

1296
00:50:02,960 --> 00:50:07,520
So now let's talk about how the data are

1297
00:50:05,119 --> 00:50:10,400
actually collected in the kspace. When

1298
00:50:07,520 --> 00:50:12,400
time t equals zero, the k value is also

1299
00:50:10,400 --> 00:50:14,640
zero, which means you are sitting right

1300
00:50:12,400 --> 00:50:16,559
at the center of k space. As time

1301
00:50:14,640 --> 00:50:18,400
increases during the frequency encoding

1302
00:50:16,559 --> 00:50:20,720
readout, the trajectory moves gradually

1303
00:50:18,400 --> 00:50:22,400
toward the right hand side along the kx

1304
00:50:20,720 --> 00:50:24,079
direction.

1305
00:50:22,400 --> 00:50:26,480
In this way, you begin to cover half of

1306
00:50:24,079 --> 00:50:28,319
the forier domain. Now remember, for

1307
00:50:26,480 --> 00:50:30,400
real valued functions, the furier

1308
00:50:28,319 --> 00:50:32,400
transform has a very useful symmetry

1309
00:50:30,400 --> 00:50:34,559
property. If you only collect data from

1310
00:50:32,400 --> 00:50:36,559
one half of kspace, the other half can

1311
00:50:34,559 --> 00:50:38,720
be inferred mathematically. This

1312
00:50:36,559 --> 00:50:41,200
symmetry reduces the amount of data we

1313
00:50:38,720 --> 00:50:43,280
need, but in practice, MRI systems still

1314
00:50:41,200 --> 00:50:45,040
often collect both halves to account for

1315
00:50:43,280 --> 00:50:47,040
noise and to achieve cleaner

1316
00:50:45,040 --> 00:50:49,200
reconstructions.

1317
00:50:47,040 --> 00:50:51,200
This diagram shows how the data lines

1318
00:50:49,200 --> 00:50:52,880
are filled. Each horizontal line

1319
00:50:51,200 --> 00:50:54,559
corresponds to a readout under the

1320
00:50:52,880 --> 00:50:57,119
frequency encoding gradient, which

1321
00:50:54,559 --> 00:50:58,880
traces across kx. After finishing one

1322
00:50:57,119 --> 00:51:01,200
line, you step the phase encoding

1323
00:50:58,880 --> 00:51:03,200
gradient to a new value, moving up or

1324
00:51:01,200 --> 00:51:06,480
down in the ky direction, and then

1325
00:51:03,200 --> 00:51:08,559
record another frequency encoded line.

1326
00:51:06,480 --> 00:51:10,400
So for example, line one at the bottom

1327
00:51:08,559 --> 00:51:12,720
corresponds to the maximum negative

1328
00:51:10,400 --> 00:51:15,040
value of the phase encoding gradient.

1329
00:51:12,720 --> 00:51:16,880
Then you step gradually upward line by

1330
00:51:15,040 --> 00:51:19,520
line until you reach the maximum

1331
00:51:16,880 --> 00:51:21,839
positive value of ky. The spacing

1332
00:51:19,520 --> 00:51:23,760
between these lines is delta ky and the

1333
00:51:21,839 --> 00:51:26,720
spacing along the frequency encoding

1334
00:51:23,760 --> 00:51:28,800
direction is delta kx. By repeating this

1335
00:51:26,720 --> 00:51:32,000
process, you fill up the entire kspace

1336
00:51:28,800 --> 00:51:34,480
grid point by point, line by line.

1337
00:51:32,000 --> 00:51:37,359
This is why we call it kspace. Each

1338
00:51:34,480 --> 00:51:39,760
coordinate kx and ky corresponds to a

1339
00:51:37,359 --> 00:51:41,839
furier component of the image. Once the

1340
00:51:39,760 --> 00:51:43,760
kspace is fully sampled, an inverse

1341
00:51:41,839 --> 00:51:45,599
furier transform is applied and that

1342
00:51:43,760 --> 00:51:47,680
directly reconstructs the image in the

1343
00:51:45,599 --> 00:51:50,319
spatial domain.

1344
00:51:47,680 --> 00:51:52,720
So the essential idea is very simple but

1345
00:51:50,319 --> 00:51:55,280
very powerful. MRI does not record the

1346
00:51:52,720 --> 00:51:57,760
image directly. Instead, it records data

1347
00:51:55,280 --> 00:51:59,520
in k space which is really the forier

1348
00:51:57,760 --> 00:52:01,520
space and then uses the inverse

1349
00:51:59,520 --> 00:52:05,200
transform to bring everything back into

1350
00:52:01,520 --> 00:52:05,200
an image we can see.

1351
00:52:06,640 --> 00:52:11,040
And here you see another important point

1352
00:52:08,559 --> 00:52:12,800
full coverage in Kspace. If we look at

1353
00:52:11,040 --> 00:52:14,800
this figure, you can see how we can

1354
00:52:12,800 --> 00:52:16,559
slightly modify the pulse sequence to

1355
00:52:14,800 --> 00:52:18,800
make sure the entire Ksp space is

1356
00:52:16,559 --> 00:52:21,040
covered symmetrically.

1357
00:52:18,800 --> 00:52:22,880
Normally during frequency encoding, you

1358
00:52:21,040 --> 00:52:24,880
simply start your data collection right

1359
00:52:22,880 --> 00:52:27,440
away. That means your measurement begins

1360
00:52:24,880 --> 00:52:29,680
at time t equal to zero. So the kx

1361
00:52:27,440 --> 00:52:32,000
trajectory also starts at zero and moves

1362
00:52:29,680 --> 00:52:33,839
only into the positive side. This means

1363
00:52:32,000 --> 00:52:36,640
by default you are not covering the

1364
00:52:33,839 --> 00:52:39,200
negative part of kspace.

1365
00:52:36,640 --> 00:52:40,960
So how do we fix this?

1366
00:52:39,200 --> 00:52:42,880
Before the regular frequency encoding

1367
00:52:40,960 --> 00:52:45,359
and data acquisition, we apply what is

1368
00:52:42,880 --> 00:52:48,000
called a defasing gradient. This is

1369
00:52:45,359 --> 00:52:50,240
shown here as GDPhase. By applying this

1370
00:52:48,000 --> 00:52:52,319
defasing pulse, we effectively shift the

1371
00:52:50,240 --> 00:52:54,720
starting point of the kx trajectory into

1372
00:52:52,319 --> 00:52:56,559
the negative region. In other words,

1373
00:52:54,720 --> 00:52:58,559
instead of starting at zero, all the

1374
00:52:56,559 --> 00:53:01,200
frequency components now begin with a

1375
00:52:58,559 --> 00:53:02,960
negative kx value.

1376
00:53:01,200 --> 00:53:04,800
Then as time increases during the

1377
00:53:02,960 --> 00:53:06,880
readout, the trajectory sweeps across

1378
00:53:04,800 --> 00:53:09,680
kspace and eventually moves into the

1379
00:53:06,880 --> 00:53:12,800
positive side. By doing this, you cover

1380
00:53:09,680 --> 00:53:15,440
the entire rectangular region of K space

1381
00:53:12,800 --> 00:53:17,280
centered around the origin.

1382
00:53:15,440 --> 00:53:19,520
This is important because according to

1383
00:53:17,280 --> 00:53:21,520
the Nyquis sampling theorem, you need to

1384
00:53:19,520 --> 00:53:24,079
cover the full bandwidth of frequencies

1385
00:53:21,520 --> 00:53:25,839
to accurately reconstruct the image. If

1386
00:53:24,079 --> 00:53:27,680
your sampling region is too narrow, you

1387
00:53:25,839 --> 00:53:29,920
will miss some forier components and

1388
00:53:27,680 --> 00:53:32,400
that leads to aliasing or artifacts in

1389
00:53:29,920 --> 00:53:34,480
the reconstructed image.

1390
00:53:32,400 --> 00:53:36,400
By carefully choosing the defasing and

1391
00:53:34,480 --> 00:53:38,559
refasing gradients, you make sure that

1392
00:53:36,400 --> 00:53:40,240
this rectangular region in kspace is

1393
00:53:38,559 --> 00:53:42,160
wide enough to include all of the

1394
00:53:40,240 --> 00:53:43,839
significant fur components of the

1395
00:53:42,160 --> 00:53:46,480
object.

1396
00:53:43,839 --> 00:53:49,119
So the essential idea here is that MRI

1397
00:53:46,480 --> 00:53:52,160
imaging works in Kspace and by adjusting

1398
00:53:49,119 --> 00:53:54,559
the gradient pulses defasing first then

1399
00:53:52,160 --> 00:53:57,040
rephasing with frequency encoding you

1400
00:53:54,559 --> 00:54:00,559
can guarantee full coverage. This is how

1401
00:53:57,040 --> 00:54:02,800
the KSPace theorem is applied in MRI.

1402
00:54:00,559 --> 00:54:04,559
And remember in our setup phase encoding

1403
00:54:02,800 --> 00:54:06,079
is along the vertical direction while

1404
00:54:04,559 --> 00:54:08,480
frequency encoding is along the

1405
00:54:06,079 --> 00:54:10,480
horizontal direction. Together they fill

1406
00:54:08,480 --> 00:54:12,640
the kspace grid ensuring that the

1407
00:54:10,480 --> 00:54:17,000
reconstructed image contains all the

1408
00:54:12,640 --> 00:54:17,000
spatial information of the object.

1409
00:54:18,319 --> 00:54:22,240
Now let me explain this idea more

1410
00:54:20,000 --> 00:54:23,760
generally. Here towel shown in green is

1411
00:54:22,240 --> 00:54:26,000
the time you spend applying the phase

1412
00:54:23,760 --> 00:54:27,839
encoding gradient. During this phase

1413
00:54:26,000 --> 00:54:29,680
encoding you introduce a phase

1414
00:54:27,839 --> 00:54:32,319
difference across the spins but you are

1415
00:54:29,680 --> 00:54:34,480
not yet collecting any signal.

1416
00:54:32,319 --> 00:54:36,480
After that you let time to run shown in

1417
00:54:34,480 --> 00:54:38,720
red and that is when you actually begin

1418
00:54:36,480 --> 00:54:40,960
collecting the MR signal. This is the

1419
00:54:38,720 --> 00:54:43,680
formulation with both phase encoding and

1420
00:54:40,960 --> 00:54:45,280
frequency encoding included.

1421
00:54:43,680 --> 00:54:47,280
We first perform phase encoding

1422
00:54:45,280 --> 00:54:49,839
longitudinally then we do frequency

1423
00:54:47,280 --> 00:54:51,920
encoding horizontally. But in fact you

1424
00:54:49,839 --> 00:54:53,920
can do this in different ways. You can

1425
00:54:51,920 --> 00:54:57,119
apply gradients in both the vertical and

1426
00:54:53,920 --> 00:54:59,280
horizontal directions simultaneously.

1427
00:54:57,119 --> 00:55:01,040
When you do this during the time towel,

1428
00:54:59,280 --> 00:55:03,440
you move the starting phase point

1429
00:55:01,040 --> 00:55:05,520
diagonally in case depending on the

1430
00:55:03,440 --> 00:55:06,960
relative strength of the gradients. If

1431
00:55:05,520 --> 00:55:09,119
you then start collecting signal

1432
00:55:06,960 --> 00:55:11,520
immediately, the case trajectory begins

1433
00:55:09,119 --> 00:55:13,599
from that shifted point.

1434
00:55:11,520 --> 00:55:15,680
Alternatively, you might first move the

1435
00:55:13,599 --> 00:55:17,680
starting point horizontally by applying

1436
00:55:15,680 --> 00:55:19,680
one gradient, then switch to a vertical

1437
00:55:17,680 --> 00:55:21,440
gradient and move to another position

1438
00:55:19,680 --> 00:55:23,440
and only then begin collecting the

1439
00:55:21,440 --> 00:55:26,720
signal. Each different choice changes

1440
00:55:23,440 --> 00:55:28,720
the trajectory you trace in kspace.

1441
00:55:26,720 --> 00:55:31,040
For example, if you use an X gradient

1442
00:55:28,720 --> 00:55:33,040
for frequency encoding, then as time t

1443
00:55:31,040 --> 00:55:35,040
increases from zero, the kx value

1444
00:55:33,040 --> 00:55:36,640
changes continuously toward positive

1445
00:55:35,040 --> 00:55:38,880
values.

1446
00:55:36,640 --> 00:55:41,040
If instead you first apply a horizontal

1447
00:55:38,880 --> 00:55:43,839
gradient as a phase encoding step, the

1448
00:55:41,040 --> 00:55:46,160
initial point shifts along the kxis.

1449
00:55:43,839 --> 00:55:48,319
Then by applying a vertical gradient,

1450
00:55:46,160 --> 00:55:50,319
you move the starting point vertically.

1451
00:55:48,319 --> 00:55:52,400
Once you begin signal acquisition, you

1452
00:55:50,319 --> 00:55:54,160
trace a horizontal or vertical line in

1453
00:55:52,400 --> 00:55:56,400
kspace.

1454
00:55:54,160 --> 00:55:58,319
Finally, if you apply both gradients at

1455
00:55:56,400 --> 00:56:00,319
once and immediately collect the signal,

1456
00:55:58,319 --> 00:56:02,160
you start at the origin and move along a

1457
00:56:00,319 --> 00:56:05,119
straight line at a slope determined by

1458
00:56:02,160 --> 00:56:07,040
the ratio of the two gradients.

1459
00:56:05,119 --> 00:56:08,880
The essential idea is that whenever you

1460
00:56:07,040 --> 00:56:10,720
collect a signal with a gradient on, the

1461
00:56:08,880 --> 00:56:12,640
frequency of procession is shifted and

1462
00:56:10,720 --> 00:56:14,559
that frequency determines the trajectory

1463
00:56:12,640 --> 00:56:16,160
in kspace.

1464
00:56:14,559 --> 00:56:17,680
If you apply a gradient without

1465
00:56:16,160 --> 00:56:20,160
collecting a signal, the spins

1466
00:56:17,680 --> 00:56:22,480
accumulate phase instead. By combining

1467
00:56:20,160 --> 00:56:24,559
these options, you can navigate Kspace

1468
00:56:22,480 --> 00:56:27,280
in virtually any pattern. This

1469
00:56:24,559 --> 00:56:29,280
flexibility is what allows MRI to encode

1470
00:56:27,280 --> 00:56:34,040
spatial information and build an image

1471
00:56:29,280 --> 00:56:34,040
slice by slice, pixel by pixel.

1472
00:56:35,280 --> 00:56:38,559
At this point, you might be wondering,

1473
00:56:36,960 --> 00:56:40,640
have you understood this or are you

1474
00:56:38,559 --> 00:56:42,400
confused? Honestly, if you have never

1475
00:56:40,640 --> 00:56:44,640
studied this carefully before, it is

1476
00:56:42,400 --> 00:56:46,799
very natural to be confused. Even for

1477
00:56:44,640 --> 00:56:48,480
me, when I first studied this, I had to

1478
00:56:46,799 --> 00:56:50,640
go through it several times very

1479
00:56:48,480 --> 00:56:53,200
carefully before I felt I understood it

1480
00:56:50,640 --> 00:56:55,040
clearly.

1481
00:56:53,200 --> 00:56:57,119
So, let's check ourselves. If you

1482
00:56:55,040 --> 00:56:59,440
basically follow the logical flow so

1483
00:56:57,119 --> 00:57:01,520
far, you can feel confident. If you are

1484
00:56:59,440 --> 00:57:03,280
quite confused, that's also expected

1485
00:57:01,520 --> 00:57:05,280
because these concepts are tricky and

1486
00:57:03,280 --> 00:57:07,040
require repeated review. If you're

1487
00:57:05,280 --> 00:57:09,040
somewhere in between, that's normal,

1488
00:57:07,040 --> 00:57:10,720
too. The important thing is not to get

1489
00:57:09,040 --> 00:57:12,960
discouraged.

1490
00:57:10,720 --> 00:57:14,880
What I will do now is go over the same

1491
00:57:12,960 --> 00:57:16,880
ideas again but from a slightly

1492
00:57:14,880 --> 00:57:18,880
different angle with more detail and

1493
00:57:16,880 --> 00:57:22,359
more illustrations so that the picture

1494
00:57:18,880 --> 00:57:22,359
becomes clearer.

1495
00:57:23,520 --> 00:57:28,160
Now let's revisit spin echo with another

1496
00:57:25,760 --> 00:57:30,799
illustration. Spin echo is a very clever

1497
00:57:28,160 --> 00:57:32,720
but also highly simplified signal model.

1498
00:57:30,799 --> 00:57:34,559
In this figure you can see the sequence

1499
00:57:32,720 --> 00:57:36,799
step by step. You begin with the

1500
00:57:34,559 --> 00:57:38,799
magnetization vector aligned along the

1501
00:57:36,799 --> 00:57:41,520
z-axis.

1502
00:57:38,799 --> 00:57:43,440
Then you apply a 90° pulse which flips

1503
00:57:41,520 --> 00:57:45,599
the vector into the transverse plane.

1504
00:57:43,440 --> 00:57:47,200
Here shown along the y-axis, though it

1505
00:57:45,599 --> 00:57:49,200
could just as well be flipped along the

1506
00:57:47,200 --> 00:57:52,079
x-axis depending on how the pulse is

1507
00:57:49,200 --> 00:57:54,160
applied. Once in the transverse plane,

1508
00:57:52,079 --> 00:57:56,960
the spins begin to defphase because of

1509
00:57:54,160 --> 00:57:58,720
local field inhomogeneities. Some spins

1510
00:57:56,960 --> 00:58:00,559
process slightly faster and others

1511
00:57:58,720 --> 00:58:02,480
slightly slower. Over time, this

1512
00:58:00,559 --> 00:58:05,440
produces a spread in phase as shown

1513
00:58:02,480 --> 00:58:07,520
here. Then comes the magic step. A 180°

1514
00:58:05,440 --> 00:58:09,920
pulse is applied.

1515
00:58:07,520 --> 00:58:11,920
This pulse flips the spins over so that

1516
00:58:09,920 --> 00:58:13,440
those that were lagging are now ahead

1517
00:58:11,920 --> 00:58:15,440
and those that were ahead are now

1518
00:58:13,440 --> 00:58:17,839
behind. From that point forward, the

1519
00:58:15,440 --> 00:58:19,359
phase differences begin to refocus.

1520
00:58:17,839 --> 00:58:20,960
After the same amount of time has

1521
00:58:19,359 --> 00:58:23,599
passed, the spins come back into

1522
00:58:20,960 --> 00:58:25,200
alignment forming an echo.

1523
00:58:23,599 --> 00:58:27,680
This is the echo signal that can be

1524
00:58:25,200 --> 00:58:29,920
detected by the coil. Importantly, this

1525
00:58:27,680 --> 00:58:31,839
echo arises from the selected slice,

1526
00:58:29,920 --> 00:58:33,680
assuming you have already applied slice

1527
00:58:31,839 --> 00:58:36,480
selection.

1528
00:58:33,680 --> 00:58:38,720
The equation shown here, equation 423,

1529
00:58:36,480 --> 00:58:40,480
expresses the signal mathematically as

1530
00:58:38,720 --> 00:58:43,200
being proportional to the integral of

1531
00:58:40,480 --> 00:58:45,520
the proton density over the slice. In

1532
00:58:43,200 --> 00:58:48,000
other words, the echo represents the

1533
00:58:45,520 --> 00:58:50,640
combined contribution of all the spins

1534
00:58:48,000 --> 00:58:52,240
in that slice after refocusing has

1535
00:58:50,640 --> 00:58:54,640
occurred.

1536
00:58:52,240 --> 00:58:56,720
So, spin echo illustrates in a very

1537
00:58:54,640 --> 00:58:59,040
clear way how we can recover useful

1538
00:58:56,720 --> 00:59:01,440
signal despite defasing and how

1539
00:58:59,040 --> 00:59:02,880
gradients and RF pulses can be combined

1540
00:59:01,440 --> 00:59:07,079
to control the evolution of

1541
00:59:02,880 --> 00:59:07,079
magnetization in MRI.

1542
00:59:08,559 --> 00:59:12,480
And now let's talk about the actual

1543
00:59:10,160 --> 00:59:15,200
signal we can measure in MRI which is

1544
00:59:12,480 --> 00:59:17,839
called the free induction decay or FID.

1545
00:59:15,200 --> 00:59:20,160
After applying a 90deree RF pulse, the

1546
00:59:17,839 --> 00:59:22,480
net magnetization vector is flipped into

1547
00:59:20,160 --> 00:59:24,559
the transverse plane. This transverse

1548
00:59:22,480 --> 00:59:26,480
component of magnetization is constantly

1549
00:59:24,559 --> 00:59:28,960
rotating around the main magnetic field

1550
00:59:26,480 --> 00:59:31,200
B 0. Because of this procession, the

1551
00:59:28,960 --> 00:59:34,720
changing magnetic field induces an

1552
00:59:31,200 --> 00:59:36,480
alternating electromagnetic field. And

1553
00:59:34,720 --> 00:59:39,040
as you learned in high school physics,

1554
00:59:36,480 --> 00:59:41,599
an alternating magnetic field induces an

1555
00:59:39,040 --> 00:59:43,760
electric current in a nearby coil. So if

1556
00:59:41,599 --> 00:59:45,520
we place a receiver coil close to the

1557
00:59:43,760 --> 00:59:47,680
patient, that coil will detect this

1558
00:59:45,520 --> 00:59:49,359
alternating signal.

1559
00:59:47,680 --> 00:59:51,839
However, the signal does not last

1560
00:59:49,359 --> 00:59:54,720
forever. It decays over time and this

1561
00:59:51,839 --> 00:59:56,720
decay is due to the T2 star relaxation.

1562
00:59:54,720 --> 00:59:58,559
The spins gradually lose phase coherence

1563
00:59:56,720 --> 01:00:00,480
and the signal amplitude decreases

1564
00:59:58,559 --> 01:00:02,640
exponentially.

1565
01:00:00,480 --> 01:00:04,720
What we record is not a constant sine

1566
01:00:02,640 --> 01:00:06,960
wave of fixed amplitude, but a damped

1567
01:00:04,720 --> 01:00:09,040
oscillation that fades away with time.

1568
01:00:06,960 --> 01:00:10,799
This decaying signal directly measured

1569
01:00:09,040 --> 01:00:12,720
from the whole sample or from a whole

1570
01:00:10,799 --> 01:00:16,160
slice if slice selection is applied is

1571
01:00:12,720 --> 01:00:18,640
the free induction decay or FID. This is

1572
01:00:16,160 --> 01:00:21,640
the fundamental NMR signal we detect in

1573
01:00:18,640 --> 01:00:21,640
MRI.

1574
01:00:23,040 --> 01:00:26,799
Now let's look at how we time the

1575
01:00:24,640 --> 01:00:29,040
acquisition of this signal. Two very

1576
01:00:26,799 --> 01:00:31,760
important parameters in MRI are TE and

1577
01:00:29,040 --> 01:00:33,520
TR. TE stands for echo time, which is

1578
01:00:31,760 --> 01:00:35,280
the time from the application of the RF

1579
01:00:33,520 --> 01:00:38,319
pulse to the moment we collect the peak

1580
01:00:35,280 --> 01:00:39,839
of the echo signal. TR stands for

1581
01:00:38,319 --> 01:00:42,400
repetition time, which is the time

1582
01:00:39,839 --> 01:00:44,480
between successive RF excitations. You

1583
01:00:42,400 --> 01:00:48,319
see in this diagram that we often use a

1584
01:00:44,480 --> 01:00:51,200
90° pulse followed by a 180° refocusing

1585
01:00:48,319 --> 01:00:53,760
pulse to generate a spin echo. The echo

1586
01:00:51,200 --> 01:00:56,799
forms at time TE. TR is measured from

1587
01:00:53,760 --> 01:00:59,520
one excitation pulse to the next.

1588
01:00:56,799 --> 01:01:01,440
These two parameters TE and TR are under

1589
01:00:59,520 --> 01:01:03,680
our control. They are not properties of

1590
01:01:01,440 --> 01:01:06,240
the tissue itself but technical choices

1591
01:01:03,680 --> 01:01:08,559
we make during the imaging sequence. By

1592
01:01:06,240 --> 01:01:10,640
carefully selecting TE and TR we can

1593
01:01:08,559 --> 01:01:13,359
emphasize different tissue contrasts,

1594
01:01:10,640 --> 01:01:15,920
highlight T1 waiting, T2 waiting or

1595
01:01:13,359 --> 01:01:18,079
proton density waiting. And at the same

1596
01:01:15,920 --> 01:01:21,280
time we can also balance image quality

1597
01:01:18,079 --> 01:01:23,359
with scan time. If TR is long, the

1598
01:01:21,280 --> 01:01:25,359
magnetization has more time to recover,

1599
01:01:23,359 --> 01:01:27,599
giving us strong signals, but the scan

1600
01:01:25,359 --> 01:01:29,200
will take longer. If TR is short,

1601
01:01:27,599 --> 01:01:30,960
scanning is faster, but some

1602
01:01:29,200 --> 01:01:33,839
magnetization may not have fully

1603
01:01:30,960 --> 01:01:36,480
recovered, which reduces the signal.

1604
01:01:33,839 --> 01:01:38,640
TE works similarly. Shorter echo times

1605
01:01:36,480 --> 01:01:40,720
reduce T2 contrast, but keep signal

1606
01:01:38,640 --> 01:01:42,799
strong, while longer echo times allow

1607
01:01:40,720 --> 01:01:44,799
more T2 differences to emerge, but at

1608
01:01:42,799 --> 01:01:47,440
the cost of weaker signals. These

1609
01:01:44,799 --> 01:01:49,040
parameters TE and TR are the main levers

1610
01:01:47,440 --> 01:01:52,599
we adjust to create different kinds of

1611
01:01:49,040 --> 01:01:52,599
MRI contrast.

1612
01:01:53,920 --> 01:01:58,240
And I said that you can collect a signal

1613
01:01:55,680 --> 01:02:00,400
with a nearby coil. Then if you place

1614
01:01:58,240 --> 01:02:02,480
another coil, you can also get a signal.

1615
01:02:00,400 --> 01:02:05,280
Remember the horizontal component of the

1616
01:02:02,480 --> 01:02:08,000
magnetization is a rotational vector. So

1617
01:02:05,280 --> 01:02:09,680
the signal actually has two parts.

1618
01:02:08,000 --> 01:02:12,000
It depends both on amplitude and on

1619
01:02:09,680 --> 01:02:14,240
phase. The signal detected along the

1620
01:02:12,000 --> 01:02:17,040
x-axis and the signal detected along the

1621
01:02:14,240 --> 01:02:18,880
y-axis naturally have a 90° phase

1622
01:02:17,040 --> 01:02:21,440
difference because the magnetization

1623
01:02:18,880 --> 01:02:24,000
keeps rotating. That makes the signal a

1624
01:02:21,440 --> 01:02:26,319
complex valued quantity.

1625
01:02:24,000 --> 01:02:29,200
So let's describe it mathematically. You

1626
01:02:26,319 --> 01:02:31,680
can record sx which is modulated as m0.

1627
01:02:29,200 --> 01:02:34,000
After a 90° pulse, this transverse

1628
01:02:31,680 --> 01:02:36,720
signal is subject to spin echo formation

1629
01:02:34,000 --> 01:02:38,559
and also subject to t2 decay. The sx

1630
01:02:36,720 --> 01:02:40,960
term represents the amplitude and the

1631
01:02:38,559 --> 01:02:43,520
frequency content along the x-axis.

1632
01:02:40,960 --> 01:02:45,839
Likewise, you have a s component which

1633
01:02:43,520 --> 01:02:47,680
is just the complimentary sign term.

1634
01:02:45,839 --> 01:02:51,040
