01:13:10,560 --> 01:13:16,640
furier transform of f of x and y

1900
01:13:13,440 --> 01:13:18,719
evaluated along the kx axis.

1901
01:13:16,640 --> 01:13:21,040
So in this case we are scanning a single

1902
01:13:18,719 --> 01:13:23,120
line in kspace the horizontal line

1903
01:13:21,040 --> 01:13:24,640
because the gradient field is only along

1904
01:13:23,120 --> 01:13:26,719
x.

1905
01:13:24,640 --> 01:13:29,040
This is a very special case but it makes

1906
01:13:26,719 --> 01:13:31,199
the principle clear. Our signal directly

1907
01:13:29,040 --> 01:13:33,199
corresponds to the furier components of

1908
01:13:31,199 --> 01:13:35,440
the object we are imaging. That's why

1909
01:13:33,199 --> 01:13:37,760
MRI data is often first collected in

1910
01:13:35,440 --> 01:13:43,080
kspace and then reconstructed by inverse

1911
01:13:37,760 --> 01:13:43,080
forier transform to get the final image.

1912
01:13:44,480 --> 01:13:48,320
Now let's consider the general case.

1913
01:13:46,480 --> 01:13:50,800
Instead of just gx we allow general

1914
01:13:48,320 --> 01:13:52,719
gradients along x y and z. These

1915
01:13:50,800 --> 01:13:55,199
gradients can vary with time and their

1916
01:13:52,719 --> 01:13:56,880
effects accumulate as the spins process.

1917
01:13:55,199 --> 01:13:58,480
The local procession frequency is

1918
01:13:56,880 --> 01:14:00,880
determined by the inner product of the

1919
01:13:58,480 --> 01:14:02,800
gradient vector g of t and the position

1920
01:14:00,880 --> 01:14:04,719
vector r.

1921
01:14:02,800 --> 01:14:07,280
When we integrate this over time, we get

1922
01:14:04,719 --> 01:14:09,600
a phase factor that builds up gradually.

1923
01:14:07,280 --> 01:14:12,560
This leads us to define k of t as gamma

1924
01:14:09,600 --> 01:14:14,480
over 2 pi * the time integral of g of t

1925
01:14:12,560 --> 01:14:16,880
to da.

1926
01:14:14,480 --> 01:14:19,040
In other words, k of t is a trajectory

1927
01:14:16,880 --> 01:14:21,280
through fier space controlled entirely

1928
01:14:19,040 --> 01:14:23,600
by our choice of gradient waveforms. The

1929
01:14:21,280 --> 01:14:25,920
measured signal s of t is then the

1930
01:14:23,600 --> 01:14:29,679
forier transform of the object's spin

1931
01:14:25,920 --> 01:14:32,159
density f of xyz evaluated along this

1932
01:14:29,679 --> 01:14:34,480
kspace trajectory.

1933
01:14:32,159 --> 01:14:36,640
This is the KSpace theorem. It tells us

1934
01:14:34,480 --> 01:14:39,040
that by designing gradient waveforms, we

1935
01:14:36,640 --> 01:14:41,120
can sample any path we want in kspace.

1936
01:14:39,040 --> 01:14:43,840
Straight lines, zigzags, spirals,

1937
01:14:41,120 --> 01:14:46,400
anything. That's why MRI is so flexible.

1938
01:14:43,840 --> 01:14:48,239
This general forier space formulation is

1939
01:14:46,400 --> 01:14:50,400
extremely powerful because it connects

1940
01:14:48,239 --> 01:14:52,320
what we measure directly to the spatial

1941
01:14:50,400 --> 01:14:54,960
distribution of spins inside the

1942
01:14:52,320 --> 01:14:56,719
patient. And again by inverse fur

1943
01:14:54,960 --> 01:15:00,520
transforming the collected data, we

1944
01:14:56,719 --> 01:15:00,520
reconstruct the image.

1945
01:15:02,000 --> 01:15:05,679
So at this point I need to emphasize

1946
01:15:03,840 --> 01:15:07,840
that this formulation is only

1947
01:15:05,679 --> 01:15:09,920
approximate. Where exactly do the

1948
01:15:07,840 --> 01:15:11,920
approximations come in? There are really

1949
01:15:09,920 --> 01:15:13,679
three places.

1950
01:15:11,920 --> 01:15:15,440
First, if you look at this expression,

1951
01:15:13,679 --> 01:15:17,840
you see that the function depends on

1952
01:15:15,440 --> 01:15:19,760
time t. But when we talk about the k

1953
01:15:17,840 --> 01:15:21,760
space theorem, we treat it as if the

1954
01:15:19,760 --> 01:15:24,480
whole function is fixed in space,

1955
01:15:21,760 --> 01:15:26,800
something like f of xyz and not time

1956
01:15:24,480 --> 01:15:28,719
varying. In reality, there is a time

1957
01:15:26,800 --> 01:15:31,520
dependence here. And when we ignore that

1958
01:15:28,719 --> 01:15:33,280
we are making an approximation.

1959
01:15:31,520 --> 01:15:35,520
Second you see this vector R the

1960
01:15:33,280 --> 01:15:38,159
positional vector. We treat it as if it

1961
01:15:35,520 --> 01:15:40,640
is constant fixed in space. But in

1962
01:15:38,159 --> 01:15:43,120
reality spins may move. For example

1963
01:15:40,640 --> 01:15:45,280
spins in blood keep moving. Patients may

1964
01:15:43,120 --> 01:15:47,440
also move. Even molecular motion

1965
01:15:45,280 --> 01:15:49,840
introduces some effects. But in this

1966
01:15:47,440 --> 01:15:52,080
model we ignore those motions and assume

1967
01:15:49,840 --> 01:15:54,000
R is constant. That is another

1968
01:15:52,080 --> 01:15:56,560
approximation.

1969
01:15:54,000 --> 01:15:58,159
and third the factor related to signal

1970
01:15:56,560 --> 01:16:00,159
decay.

1971
01:15:58,159 --> 01:16:02,640
In practice the signal we measure is

1972
01:16:00,159 --> 01:16:04,800
subject to T2 star decay. But in this

1973
01:16:02,640 --> 01:16:07,199
approximation we assume that the readout

1974
01:16:04,800 --> 01:16:09,600
is fast enough so that no significant T2

1975
01:16:07,199 --> 01:16:11,760
star decay occurs during acquisition.

1976
01:16:09,600 --> 01:16:13,440
That is a third approximation. Under

1977
01:16:11,760 --> 01:16:15,199
these assumptions the formulation

1978
01:16:13,440 --> 01:16:16,800
reduces neatly to a forier

1979
01:16:15,199 --> 01:16:19,600
transformation which makes image

1980
01:16:16,800 --> 01:16:21,520
reconstruction straightforward.

1981
01:16:19,600 --> 01:16:23,440
But if you want very accurate imaging,

1982
01:16:21,520 --> 01:16:25,840
all these neglected factors must be

1983
01:16:23,440 --> 01:16:27,679
included. And once you include them, the

1984
01:16:25,840 --> 01:16:30,159
reconstruction is no longer a simple

1985
01:16:27,679 --> 01:16:31,920
forier transform. It becomes much more

1986
01:16:30,159 --> 01:16:33,760
complex.

1987
01:16:31,920 --> 01:16:35,440
In fact, this is an area where machine

1988
01:16:33,760 --> 01:16:37,840
learning and advanced reconstruction

1989
01:16:35,440 --> 01:16:39,679
methods can play a big role. In the last

1990
01:16:37,840 --> 01:16:41,360
part of the course, I will mention how

1991
01:16:39,679 --> 01:16:44,920
modern approaches are being used to

1992
01:16:41,360 --> 01:16:44,920
solve these challenges.

